{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And your staff with which you struck the river.</b> It was turned to harm — <b>Take in your hand and strike</b> the rock, and it will turn to good, for the rock will turn into a pool of water." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> אשר הכית בו את היאור — three things are they, each of which pushed away punishment and brought punishment; these are the incense, and the Ark, and the staff. The incense pushed away punishment, as it says (Numbers 17:12): ״ויתן את הקטרת ויכפר על העם״ — and it brought punishment (Leviticus 10:1) upon Nadab and Abihu. The Ark — it is what killed Uzzah and the men of Beit Shemesh, as it says (1 Samuel 6:19): ״ויך באנשי בית שמש כי ראו בארון ה׳״ — and it blessed Oved-Edom the Gittite, as it says (2 Samuel 6:11): ״ויברך ה׳ את עבד אדום ואת כל ביתו״. The staff of Sanpirinon — it is what killed the firstborn of Egypt and brought the plagues upon Egypt, and it is what brought forth water from the rock. End of the Midrash." "<b>And know</b> and observe that no prophet ever made use of the staff except Moses alone. And because the staff was the beginning of the signs, it was given to Moses, who was the beginning of the prophets. And even Joshua his disciple, who received from him, did not use the staff but the spear (כידון); as it is written (Joshua 8:18): ״ויאמר ה׳ אל יהושע נטה בכידון אשר בידך על העי״. And the reason for this is that the staff that is in the hand of the ruler signifies dominion and capability and power, in the manner of what is written (Ezekiel 19:14): ״מטה עז שבט למשול״; but the spear is the practice of every one who goes out to battle, that he carries it in his hand, and this is not a sign of dominion and rulership like the staff." "<b>And behold,</b> the difference between the staff and the spear is like the difference that exists between the prophecy of Moses and the prophecy of Joshua. And the staff — its number is known, and כידון is the angel, and understand this: for the prophecy of Moses was through the actualization of the supernal power without the mediation of an angel, and the prophecy of Joshua — יהושע ה׳:י״א׳:י״א — was by means of the angel, who returned to his place after the death of Moses the servant of HaShem. And the distinction between the prophecy of Moses our teacher and Joshua is like the distinction between heaven and earth, for the one acts and the other receives. Therefore, all the days of Moses, they ate bread from heaven, as it says: ״הנני ממטיר לכם לחם מן השמים״. Once Moses our teacher died and they came under the authority of Joshua, they ate bread from the earth, as it says (Joshua 5): ״ויאכלו מעבור הארץ״. And from this matter it was that Moses assigned to Joshua the war against Amalek on earth, while he took for himself the war of heaven, to cast down the power of Amalek and uproot it from the world. And concerning the difference that exists between the prophecy of Moses and the prophecy of Joshua, the Sages of blessed memory expounded: the countenance of Moses is like the countenance of the sun, and the countenance of Joshua is like the countenance of the moon. And the explanation of this is that the distinction of Moses' prophecy derives from the Active Intellect (הפועל), and the distinction of Joshua's prophecy derives from the receiver (המקבל), for so too these luminaries, sun and moon — the one acts and the other receives."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {3 "The string יהושע ה׳:י״א׳:י״א in the source appears to be a corrupted citation or dittography; translated hyper-literally as it stands, retaining the Hebrew characters exactly as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:00:24.783279423Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}